gitrl-f (0.10.2-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * The Unstaged changes row no longer counts the lines of untracked files.
    To count them, the app read each untracked file in full. With 1 GB of
    untracked text, the window took 5.7 s to open and stopped for 5.4 s
    after each edit. It now opens in 0.3 s and does not stop.
  * The window checks for edits and new commits every 2 s. Before, the
    first check after the window opened came up to 2.75 s later.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:12:35 +0200

gitrl-f (0.10.1-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * A reload is faster when the refs do not change. With 863 refs, a reload
    takes 0.47 s, not 0.82 s.
  * The line counts of the uncommitted changes are faster. With 20298
    changed files, they take 0.96 s, not 1.38 s.
  * The search of messages, authors and hashes is faster. In 100000
    commits, Enter takes about 0.17 s, not 0.26 s, and Display matches only
    about 0.08 s, not 0.41 s.
  * The Changed lines search runs one git process for each processor. In
    100000 commits, it takes 1.96 s, not 4.13 s.
  * The diff of a commit with many files fills faster. With 20000 files,
    the first 2000 files show after 4.2 s, not 9.3 s.
  * The window no longer stops for a moment every 2 s while it checks the
    work tree for changes.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:22:35 +0200

gitrl-f (0.10.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * The Unstaged changes and Staged changes rows above the list show the
    lines that they add and remove, under the line counts column, as the
    commit rows do.
  * The added and removed counts are nearer to each other, as in "+489-54",
    and the column is 8 pixels narrower.
  * A tick redraws the history faster in a big repository. In a test
    repository, a tick of every ref takes 0.27 s, not 0.46 s.
  * The install adds gitf, a short name for gitrlf, unless another program
    called gitf is on the PATH.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:42:52 +0200

gitrl-f (0.9.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Ctrl and Shift clicks select more than one commit. The pane then shows
    one diff of only those commits, applied oldest first. The difftool
    opens this diff, or one file of it.
  * A new column after the subject shows the lines that each commit adds
    and removes. The added lines are green and the removed lines are red.
  * The text of the total in the diff pane now gives the two counts apart,
    for example "2 files in total (+72/-13)".
  * When gitrlf starts from a terminal, it gives the prompt back
    immediately. The --foreground option keeps the prompt until the window
    closes, as before.
  * If the difftool does not open, the window shows an error with the
    cause.
  * The side branch of the icon is now Olympic Blue.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:27:49 +0200

gitrl-f (0.8.2-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * A click on the commit that the details show hides the details. A click
    on a different commit selects it, and the details follow it.
  * On a commit that changes one file, a double-click shows the diff below
    the refs and the list, as on other commits. Enter continues to fill the
    window with the diff.
  * When the details close, the selected row stays lit.
  * A new search in the find bar of the diff starts at the top of the pane.
    It goes to the first match at or below that point, in an open file.
    Before, it went to the first match of the commit.
  * The labels of the staged and unstaged changes start at the same
    position as the branch labels.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 09:31:41 +0200

